-We have presented \model and a production-line defect detection system for circular knitting machines. \model couples defect morphology encoding with content-adaptive feature reconstruction for knitted-fabric defect detection. DME models horizontally elongated defects, vertically elongated defects, and localized hole-like defects in the backbone, while CAFR replaces fixed upsampling with CARAFE-based local reassembly in the top-down feature pyramid. On the production-line test set, \model improves the YOLOv8n baseline from 59.3\% to 66.3\% AP and from 94.1\% to 96.9\% AP50, with 3.39 M parameters and approximately 8.9 G FLOPs. These results, together with the component ablation, support the complementary roles of defect morphology encoding and feature reassembly in improving overall localization performance while retaining a compact model scale.
-
-The detector is deployed on an RK3566 edge device. Combined with Hall-sensor-based machine-state detection and wired stop control, the system performs local defect detection and intervention without depending on cloud communication. Detection results are displayed for on-site operator review and troubleshooting, while defect records and device states are uploaded to the cloud for equipment monitoring and production-quality analysis. This deployment connects the detector to local machine control, supporting not only offline defect detection but also in-process defect containment during circular-knitting production. Future work will focus on broader production conditions, long-term false-stop and missed-detection behavior, quantized-model accuracy, and finer-grained analysis of the proposed modules.
+This paper presents \model and a defect detection system for circular knitting machines. \model introduces defect morphology encoding (DME) to capture the spatial characteristics of horizontal defects, vertical defects, and holes. Content-adaptive feature reassembly (CAFR) improves feature reconstruction during multi-scale fusion. Experiments on the production line dataset show that \model achieves 66.3\% AP and 96.9\% AP50, outperforming YOLOv8n by 7.0 and 2.8 percentage points, respectively, with 3.39 M parameters and 8.9 G FLOPs. The ablation results further demonstrate the complementary effects of DME and CAFR.
+
+The proposed detector is deployed on an RK3566 edge device and integrated with machine monitoring and wired stop control. The system enables automatic defect detection and timely intervention without relying on cloud communication. Detection results are displayed locally for operators, while the cloud platform supports equipment monitoring and production quality analysis. By integrating defect detection with machine control and quality management, this work provides a complete and practical solution for fabric defect inspection in circular knitting production. Future work will explore the application of \model to different knitted fabrics with varying texture patterns and defect characteristics to evaluate its generalization across production conditions.
